import json,pathlib
r='frontier-40-geometry-braids-rep-27';p=pathlib.Path('research')/f'{r}-batch-25.pages.json';a=json.loads(p.read_text());src={'title':'The Stacks Project, Resolution of Surfaces, Definitions 54.5.1/54.14.1–2 and Lemma 54.5.3','url':'https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/download/resolve.pdf'}
def row(id,title,deps,statement,strategy,level=0,kind='lemma'):return dict(id=id,kind=kind,title=title,deps=deps,statement=statement,strategy=strategy,design_row='AG-BIR-1',local_addition=True,provenance={'statement':'literature-derived','proof':'not-applicable' if kind=='definition' else 'ai-altered'},sources={'references':[src]},dependency_level=level)
h1=row('def-normal-surface-modification-and-normalized-point-blowup','Normal scheme modifications and normalized point blowups',['def-normal-noetherian-ring','def-integral-scheme','def-proper-morphism','def-blowup-scheme-along-ideal','thm-integrality-commutes-with-localisation','lem-relative-spec-glues-affine-algebras'],r'''A locally Noetherian scheme is normal when every local ring is an integrally closed domain. For an integral scheme $S$, its normalization $S^\nu$ is obtained by gluing $\operatorname{Spec}\overline A$ over each nonempty affine $\operatorname{Spec}A\subset S$, where $\overline A$ is the integral closure in the common function field. A modification of integral schemes is a proper morphism inducing an isomorphism of function fields. A normalized point blowup at a closed point is its blowup followed by normalization. A regular resolution is a modification with regular source. A resolution by normalized point blowups starts with the normalization and then a finite sequence of proper normalized point blowups with regular terminal scheme. If $S$ is normal, its initial normalization is the identity. Finiteness of a normalization is an additional assertion, never part of the definition.''',r'''Integral closure commutes with localization, so the affine integral-closure algebras identify on principal overlaps inside the common function field and satisfy the cocycle identity. The relative-Spec gluing supplier constructs their scheme and canonical map. Normality is the local algebra condition. The other terms are compositions and definitions; no normalization-finiteness or resolution-existence assertion is made.''',kind='definition')
h2=row('lem-surface-modification-isomorphism-in-codimension-one','A normal-surface modification is an isomorphism in codimension one',[h1['id'],'def-axiom-of-choice','lem-normal-domain-implies-r-one','thm-one-dimensional-regular-local-rings-are-dvrs','thm-valuative-criterion-properness','cor-quasi-finite-locus-open-finite-type-algebra','thm-proper-quasi-finite-is-finite','thm-integrality-and-finite-module-equivalences','lem-proper-stable-base-change','thm-proper-morphism-closed-image'],r'''Assume AC. Let $f:X\to S$ be a modification of integral Noetherian schemes and let $S$ be normal of dimension two. Then $f$ is an isomorphism over an open subset containing every point of codimension at most one in $S$. The complement is a finite set of closed points. If every fibre is zero-dimensional, $f$ is an isomorphism. The union of curves contracted by $f$ is contained in the fibres over that finite set and is finite when $\dim X=2$.''',r'''At a codimension-one point $s$ the normal Noetherian local ring is a DVR by normality's R1 implication and the DVR supplier. Base-changing to its spectrum preserves properness and integrality (on affine charts this is localization); the generic inverse extends by the valuative criterion to a section of the proper morphism. A section of a separated morphism is a closed immersion: it is a base change of the closed diagonal. Its image contains the generic point and hence, being closed, all of the integral source; its defining ideal has radical zero and is zero because the source is reduced. Thus this base change is an isomorphism. The generic-point case is immediate. Around the fibre over $s$, the quasi-finite locus is open by its actual affine finite-type algebra supplier. The proper image of its closed complement is closed and misses $s$, so restrict the base away from that image. There the map is proper quasi-finite, hence finite. On an affine normal target its finite domain algebra lies integrally in the same fraction field, so equals the target algebra by integral closedness. This proves an isomorphism on a neighbourhood of $s$. The same construction gives the open union containing codimensions zero and one. Its closed complement is zero-dimensional in the Noetherian two-dimensional integral space, hence consists of finitely many closed points. If all fibres are zero-dimensional the whole morphism is proper quasi-finite and the same affine argument gives an isomorphism. If $\dim X=2$, each exceptional fibre is a proper closed subset of the integral two-dimensional space and has dimension at most one; it has finitely many irreducible components, and each contracted integral curve is a one-dimensional component of one of these finitely many fibres. No classical-variety finite-birational supplier is used outside its scope.''',1)
h3=row('lem-finite-over-projective-noetherian-affine-base-is-projective','Finite schemes over projective schemes are projective over a Noetherian affine base',['def-axiom-of-choice','def-finite-morphism-schemes','lem-eventual-global-generation-coherent-twists','thm-integrality-and-finite-module-equivalences','thm-relative-proj-base-change','thm-segre-line-bundle-external-tensor'],r'''Assume AC. Let $R$ be Noetherian, let $X$ be projective over $R$, and let $Y\to X$ be finite. Then $Y\to X$ admits a closed immersion into one relative projective space over $X$, and $Y$ is projective over $R$. Finite compositions of projective morphisms between such schemes are projective.''',r'''The finite algebra $F=f_*\mathcal O_Y$ is coherent. Put $E=\mathcal O_X\oplus F$. Evaluation gives $Y\to\mathbb P_X(E)$, entirely in the chart where the distinguished unit coordinate $z_0$ is nonzero. On an affine $\operatorname{Spec}A\subset X$, choose finite algebra generators $b_i$ for the finite module algebra $B$. Define the graded quotient with degree zero $A$ and every positive degree $B$, sending $z_0$ to $1$ and $z_i$ to $b_i$. Each integral equation for $b_i$ homogenizes to an equation $z_i^{n_i}+\sum_{j<n_i}a_{ij}z_i^jz_0^{n_i-j}=0$. A homogeneous prime of this quotient containing $z_0$ would contain every coordinate, hence is irrelevant. Thus its Proj is wholly in the distinguished affine chart and is precisely $\operatorname{Spec}B$ there. These equations glue by localization, giving a closed immersion $Y\hookrightarrow\mathbb P_X(E)$. Let $L$ be the very ample bundle of a fixed $R$-projective embedding of $X$. Eventual generation gives finite global generators of $E\otimes L^m$ for some $m$: the module of global sections is finite by projective coherent finiteness, or one finite generating subfamily is obtained from a finite cover. Their surjection from a finite free sheaf embeds its projectivization in one $\mathbb P_X^q$. Tensoring a coherent bundle by an invertible sheaf does not change its quotient projectivization, so $Y\hookrightarrow\mathbb P_X^q$. Combining with $X\hookrightarrow\mathbb P_R^N$ and the relative Segre embedding makes $Y$ projective over $R$. For two projective morphisms, their embeddings into successive relative projective spaces combine via the same relative product and Segre construction. This proves exactly the arbitrary Noetherian affine-base extension needed for finite normalizations, rather than importing the existing field-only statement.''')
for h in [h1,h2,h3]:
 idx=next(i for i,x in enumerate(a[0]['items']) if x['id']=='thm-resolution-of-normal-surface-singularities');a[0]['items'].insert(idx,h)
